            <div class="subject">
                    <span>
                            Constitutional law<br/>
                    </span>
            </div> 
                <p class="snippet" >
                    Per McLachlin C.J.: Applying the revised Morin framework (R. v. Morin, [1992] 1 S.C.R. 771) proposed by the minority in R. v. Jordan, <b>2016 SCC 27</b>, [2016] 1 S.C.R. 631, the Court of Appeal did not err in entering a stay of proceedings. &nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp;Applied: R. v. Morin, [1992] 1 S.C.R. 771; referred to: R. v. Jordan, <b>2016 SCC 27</b>, [2016] 1 S.C.R. 631. By Cromwell J. (dissenting) R. v. Jordan, <b>2016 SCC 27</b>, [2016] 1 S.C.R. 631; R. v. Morin, [1992] 1 S.C.R. 771; R. v. Ralph, 2014 ONCA 3, 299 C.R.R. (2d) 1; R. v. Steele, 2012 ONCA 383, 288 C.C.C. (3d) 255. &nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp;[2] In the companion appeal of R. v. Jordan, <b>2016 SCC 27</b>, [2016] 1 S.C.R. 631, we stated a new framework for determining whether s. 11(b) has been infringed. 
                </p>
