            </div> 
            <div class="subject">
            </div> 
                <p class="snippet" >
                    Permitting parties access to certiorari review for an error of law on the face of the record, in particular in an evidentiary ruling, gives rise to de facto interlocutory appeals and is in direct tension with the approach set out in R. v. Jordan, <b>2016 SCC 27</b>, [2016] 1 S.C.R. 631, to achieve prompt justice in criminal cases.&nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp; 1 S.C.R. 366; R. v. Deschamplain, 2004 SCC 76, [2004] 3 S.C.R. 601; R. v. Cunningham, 2010 SCC 10, [2010] 1 S.C.R. 331; R. v. Primeau, [1995] 2 S.C.R. 60; R. v. Black, 2011 ABCA 349, 286 C.C.C. (3d) 432; R. v. Jordan, <b>2016 SCC 27</b>, [2016] 1 S.C.R. 631; R. v. Chaplin, [1995] 1 S.C.R. 727. Statutes and Regulations Cited &nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp;Such a rule would be in direct tension with the approach set out in R. v. Jordan, <b>2016 SCC 27</b>, [2016] 1 S.C.R. 631, to achieve prompt justice in criminal cases. 
                </p>
