</li>
    <li class="odd list-item-expanded">
<div class="number">
    <span class="digits">11.</span>
</div>
<div class="metadata">
    <div class="info" >
        <div class="subinfo">
            <h3>
                <span class="title"><a target="_parent" href="/scc-csc/scc-csc/en/item/19349/index.do?q=%222016+SCC+27%22">R. v. J.F.</a></span>
                        - <span class="citation">2022 SCC 17</span>
                    - <span class="report-citation">[2022] 1 SCR 330</span>
                - <span class="publicationDate">2022-05-06</span>
            </h3>
            <div class="subMetadata">
                <span>Supreme Court Judgments</span>
            </div> 
            <div class="subject">
                    <span>
                            Constitutional law<br/>
                    </span>
            </div> 
                <p class="snippet" >
                    R. v. Jordan, <b>2016 SCC 27</b>, [2016] 1 S.C.R. 631; R. v. Rahey, [1987] 1 S.C.R. 588; R. v. Cody, 2017 SCC 31, [2017] 1 S.C.R. 659; R. v. Morin, [1992] 1 S.C.R. 771; R. v. Rabba (1991), 64 C.C.C. (3d) 445; M.G. v. R., 2019 QCCA 1170. &nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp;[1] The Chief Justice — In R. v. Jordan, <b>2016 SCC 27</b>, [2016] 1 S.C.R. 631, this Court delivered a clear message to all participants in the criminal justice system in Canada: everyone must take proactive measures to prevent delay and to uphold the right to be tried within a reasonable time guaranteed to an accused by s. &nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp;[81] This appeal concerns the interaction between the culture shift introduced by this Court since R. v. Jordan, <b>2016 SCC 27</b>, [2016] 1 S.C.R. 631, the presumptive ceilings within which an accused must be brought to trial, and the situation — not contemplated by Jordan — in which a new trial is ordered. 
                </p>
