        </div> 
    </div> 
    </div> 
</li>
    <li class="even list-item-expanded">
<div class="number">
    <span class="digits">24.</span>
</div>
<div class="metadata">
    <div class="info" >
        <div class="subinfo">
            <h3>
                <span class="title"><a target="_parent" href="/scc-csc/scc-csc/en/item/17634/index.do?q=%222016+SCC+27%22">R. v. Myers</a></span>
                        - <span class="citation">2019 SCC 18</span>
                    - <span class="report-citation">[2019] 2 SCR 105</span>
                - <span class="publicationDate">2019-03-28</span>
            </h3>
            <div class="subMetadata">
                <span>Supreme Court Judgments</span>
            </div> 
            <div class="subject">
                    <span>
                            Criminal law<br/>
                    </span>
            </div> 
                <p class="snippet" >
                     Limited Partnership v. Rex, 2002 SCC 42, [2002] 2 S.C.R. 559; R. v. Morales, [1992] 3 S.C.R. 711; R. v. Bray (1983), 40 O.R. (2d) 766; R. v. Jordan, <b>2016 SCC 27</b>, [2016] 1 S.C.R. 631; Fraser Regional Correctional Centre v. Canada (Attorney General), 1993 CanLII 354; R. v. Pearson, [1992] 3 S.C.R. 665; R. v. Anoussis,&nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp;This general guiding premise has not been displaced by R. v. Jordan, <b>2016 SCC 27</b>, [2016] 1 S.C.R. 631. Sections 525(9) and 526, which confer on the reviewing judge a discretion to give directions for expediting the trial of and any proceedings in relation to an accused, continue to operate as a reflection of that principle.
                </p>
