                <p class="snippet" >
                    167; R. v. Parisien (1971), 3 C.C.C. (2d) 433; R. v. R.V., 2021 SCC 10, [2021] 1 S.C.R. 131; R. v. Sciascia, 2017 SCC 57, [2017] 2 S.C.R. 539; R. v. Jordan, <b>2016 SCC 27</b>, [2016] 1 S.C.R. 631; R. v. Wong, 2018 SCC 25, [2018] 1 S.C.R. 696; R. v. Eizenga, 2011 ONCA 113, 270 C.C.C. (3d) 168; R. v. Shin, 2015 ONCA 189, 322 C.C.C.&nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp;In this regard, “[s]creening out marginal charges that add complexity is a particularly important function given the strains of our overburdened criminal justice system” (R. v. Sciascia, 2017 SCC 57, [2017] 2 S.C.R. 539, at para. 32, citing R. v. Jordan, <b>2016 SCC 27</b>, [2016] 1 S.C.R. 631, at para. 79). The withdrawal of a 
                </p>
